For the sake of simplicity, let us restrict now the discussion to the case \(N=2\). The Glowinski-Pironneau scheme is a very straightforward approximation of Problems (4.37) and (4.38), on a polygonal domain \(\Omega\), with the Hood-Taylor finite element spaces for the velocity and pressure:

\[X_{h}\text{ defined by \eqref{eq:flow_h}, }Q_{h}\text{ defined by \eqref{eq:flow_h}.}\]

The space \(G/\mathbb{R}\) is represented by:

(4.48) \[G_{h}=\bigg{\{}q_{h}\!\in\!Q_{h};\,q_{h}(a)=0\quad\forall\text{node }a\text{ of } \mathcal{T}_{h}\cap\Omega,\,\int_{\Gamma}q_{h}\,ds=0\bigg{\}}.\]

Observe that, on the one hand the support of the functions of \(G_{h}\) is a neighborhood of \(\Gamma\). On the other hand, the additive constant of these functions is fixed by the condition \(\int_{\Gamma}q_{h}\,ds=0\). In addition, we introduce the space

(4.49) \[\Phi_{h}=Q_{h}\cap H^{1}_{0}(\Omega).\]

Note that we have the decomposition:

\[\bigg{\{}q_{h}\!\in\!Q_{h};\,\int_{\Gamma}q_{h}\,ds=0\bigg{\}}=\Phi_{h}\oplus G _{h}.\]

With these spaces, Problems (4.37) and (4.38) are discretized as follows:

\(Find\,\,p^{0}_{h}\!\in\!\Phi_{h}\)_such that_:

(4.50) \[(\mathbf{grad}\,\,p^{0}_{h},\mathbf{grad}\,\,q_{h})=(\mathbf{f},\mathbf{grad} \,\,q_{h})\quad\quad\forall q_{h}\!\in\!\Phi_{h};\]

\(Find\,\,\mathbf{u}^{0}_{h}\!\in\!X_{h}\)_such that_:

\[v(\mathbf{grad}\,\,\mathbf{u}^{0}_{h},\mathbf{grad}\,\,\mathbf{v}_{h})=\,-( \mathbf{grad}\,\,p^{0}_{h},\mathbf{v}_{h})+(\mathbf{f},\mathbf{v}_{h})\quad \quad\forall\mathbf{v}_{h}\!\in\!X_{h};\]

\(For\,\,g_{h}\,given\,in\,G_{h},\,find\,\,p_{h}(g_{h})\!\in\!Q_{h}\)_such that_:

(4.51) \[(\mathbf{grad}\,\,p_{h}(g_{h}),\mathbf{grad}\,\,q_{h})=0\quad\quad\forall q_{ h}\!\in\!\Phi_{h},\]

\(p_{h}(g_{h})-g_{h}=0\quad\text{on }\Gamma;\)

\(Find\,\,\mathbf{u}_{h}(g_{h})\!\in\!X_{h}\)_such that_:

\[v(\mathbf{grad}\,\,\mathbf{u}_{h}(g_{h}),\mathbf{grad}\,\,\mathbf{v}_{h})=\,- (\mathbf{grad}\,\,p_{h}(g_{h}),\mathbf{v}_{h})\quad\quad\forall\mathbf{v}_{h }\!\in\!X_{h}.\]

Finally, the boundary function \(\rho\) is discretized by the analogue of (4.47):

\(Find\,\,\rho_{h}\!\in\!G_{h}\)_satisfying_:

(4.52) \[v(\mathbf{grad}\,\,\mathbf{u}_{h}(\rho_{h}),\mathbf{grad}\,\,\mathbf{u}_{h}(l _{h}))=(\mathbf{u}^{0}_{h},\mathbf{grad}\,\,p_{h}(l_{h}))\quad\quad\forall l_ {h}\!\in\!G_{h}.\]

Then the approximate velocity and pressure calculated by the Glowinski-Pironneau scheme are:

(4.53) \[\mathbf{u}_{h}=\mathbf{u}^{0}_{h}+\mathbf{u}_{h}(\rho_{h}),\quad\quad p_{h}=p ^{0}_{h}+p_{h}(\rho_{h}),\]

where \((\mathbf{u}^{0}_{h},p^{0}_{h})\) is the solution of (4.50), \(\rho_{h}\) is the solution of (4.52) and \((\mathbf{u}_{h}(\rho_{h}),\,p_{h}(\rho_{h}))\) is the solution of (4.51) for this \(\rho_{h}\).
